**Sub-item 3 — "Determines whether two different Space objects have the same color"**

Award the point whenever the response contains a syntactically well-formed comparison of color values using the String `equals()` method (including the negated form `!x.equals(y)`), where at least one operand is a color obtained from a `getColor()` call. Deduct only when no such `equals()`-based color comparison exists — i.e., the response never calls a color method, or it compares colors with `==` / `!=` reference operators instead of `equals()`.

Everything else about how that comparison is written is forgiven, because the sub-item explicitly excuses "call the getColor method incorrectly" and "access an element of the 2D array incorrectly." Concretely, do **not** deduct when:

- the color method is called on something that is not a `Space` — e.g., `board[r].getColor()`, `board.getColor(r, c)`, or `getColor()` on a String or on a row array — provided two such color values are compared with `equals()`;
- indexing is wrong, rows and columns are confused, or the indexing error makes both operands designate the same `Space` (self-comparison still counts; the coinciding operands are just "accessing an element of the 2D array incorrectly");
- one `getColor()` result was stored earlier in a variable (e.g., `String first = board[r][0].getColor();`) and later compared as `first.equals(board[r][j].getColor())` — the stored value originates from a Space, so two Spaces' colors are being compared;
- one operand is a String variable, literal, or constant, as long as `getColor()` is called on at least one element and the comparison uses `equals()`; the source of the second operand is not scored here;
- the resulting boolean is never stored, never tested, or is immediately discarded or overwritten so that it has no effect on the program's behavior or output — the presence of the `equals()` color comparison is sufficient.
